and they all sort of collaborate with each other to make sure that their money goes for the topics that they're most interested in. Let's talk about the most recent focus of the study, which is aging. have developed a test called Dunedin Pace, which measures the rate of people's biological aging. Tell us more about what that means. What's biological aging? How does the test work? So one of the important things to think about biological aging is quite obvious. It's different from chronological aging. So you can have a group of people, as we have all of the Dunedin study members born in 1972, 73, they're all exactly the same age, but their biological age can range, you know, 10 or 15 years around their chronological age.

